ands\n- Being suited adds significant value, giving me flush potential and making this a premium suited connector\n- In heads-up Texas Hold'em, this is definitely a playable and often profitable hand\n\n**Possible Actions:**\n1. **Check (0):** This is the small blind position, so checking would let me see the flop cheaply. However, this is passive and doesn't build the pot with a strong hand.\n\n2. **Fold (-1):** This would be extremely weak with K-T suited. This hand has significant equity and folding would be a major mistake.\n\n3. **Raise (200+):** This would be a standard aggressive play to:\n   - Build the pot with a strong hand\n   - Put pressure on opponent\n   - Take initiative in the hand\n   - Potentially win immediately if opponent folds\n\n4. **All-in (-2):** This is too aggressive pre-flop with K-T suited. While it's a good hand, it's not strong enough to risk all chips before seeing any community cards.\n\n**Strategic Consideration:**\n- I'm down 100 chips cumulatively (though that's independent)\n- Currently have 19,950 chips\n- K-T suited plays well post-flop with multiple possibilities (pair, flush, straight draws)\n- A standard raise of 200-400 chips (3-4x big blind equivalent) would be appropriate\n\nReason:\nK-T suited is a premium hand in heads-up play that warrants aggressive action. A raise accomplishes multiple objectives: it builds the pot when I likely have the best hand, allows me to take control of the betting, and gives me information about my opponent's hand strength. Checking would be too passive with such a strong holding, while going all-in would be overcommitting with a hand that, while strong, isn't a premium pair.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
